# Normalize line endings: LF in the repository, native on checkout where it
# matters. The build/tooling scripts require LF.
* text=auto
*.sh text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
# PowerShell scripts get CRLF and are stored WITH a UTF-8 BOM: Windows
# PowerShell 5.1 (the version shipped with Windows) decodes a BOM-less file as
# ANSI, which turns the em dashes in these scripts into U+201D — a character
# PowerShell treats as a string delimiter, so the scripts fail to parse.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.sdoc text
*.cpp text
*.h text
*.md text
*.json text
*.yml text

# Generated file, committed for GitHub/Doxygen browsing — regenerated by
# tools/make_requirements.sh; mark so diffs de-emphasize it.
#
# eol=lf is load-bearing: tools/sdoc_to_md.py writes LF on both platforms (so
# the two produce byte-identical output). Without it, git checks the file out
# with CRLF on Windows, every regeneration there rewrites it to LF, and the file
# shows up as permanently modified.
docs/requirements.md linguist-generated=true text eol=lf
